  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the solution of $-{x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+3x\n$$\n\nlies *above* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ -x(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/68d82c96c820a6a0bf5e3c74ea93cbf2091e96ebfa7dfe200e8beb140cb7962e.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *above* the axis or touches the $x$-axis. \n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $0\\le x\\le 3.$",
